❓Question
Given a string text, use the characters of text to form as many instances of the word "balloon" as possible.
Each character can be used at most once. Return the maximum number of "balloon" that can be formed.

Example 1
Input: text = "nlaebolko"
Output: 1

xample 2
Input: text = "loonbalxballpoon"
Output: 2

✅Approach
Create a HashMap to count only the characters needed for "balloon".
Count b, a, l, o, and n.
Each "balloon" needs:
1 b
1 a
2 l
2 o
1 n
Therefore, divide the count of l and o by 2.
The character with the smallest available count determines how many "balloon" words can be formed.

Code
class Solution {
    public int maxNumberOfBalloons(String text) {
        HashMap<Character, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for(int i = 0; i < text.length(); i++){
            char ch = text.charAt(i);
            if(ch == 'b' || ch == 'a' || ch == 'l' ||
               ch == 'o' || ch == 'n'){
                map.put(ch, map.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);
            }
        }
        int b = map.getOrDefault('b', 0);
        int a = map.getOrDefault('a', 0);
        int l = map.getOrDefault('l', 0) / 2;
        int o = map.getOrDefault('o', 0) / 2;
        int n = map.getOrDefault('n', 0);
        return Math.min(Math.min(Math.min(b, a), Math.min(l, o)),n);
    }
}

Time Complexity
O(n)
Space Complexity
O(1)
